course: technology
lesson: 1
title: How a factory works: belts, robots, checks and people
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A factory is a building where the same thing is made again and again, thousands of times a day, by machines and people working in a fixed order.
explain: The things ride along on a conveyor belt, so each machine or worker does one small job and passes the thing on, like a relay race where the baton is a biscuit or a bottle.
explain: Your chocolate bar was probably never touched by a bare hand: machines mixed, shaped, wrapped and boxed it, while people in hairnets watched the machines, fixed jams and tasted samples.
explain: Cameras and metal detectors check every item, and anything wrong is knocked off the line before it can reach a shop.
explain: Machines are strong and fast and cannot see you, so factories are full of fences, guards and big red stop buttons, and visitors keep to the marked walkways.
fact: A conveyor belt is a long loop of rubber or metal pulled round by motors and rollers, carrying each item past the machines and workers in order.
fact: In 1913 Henry Ford's moving assembly line cut the time to build a car from about twelve hours to about an hour and a half.
fact: Belts and rollers can pull in hair, sleeves and fingers, so workers tie hair back, wear close-fitting clothes and stop the machine before touching it, never using their hands on a moving belt.
fact: An emergency stop button cuts the power at once and stays pressed in until someone has checked the problem and twisted it out; it is usually red on a yellow background.
fact: In a factory, anyone who sees something going wrong may press the emergency stop, even a visitor.
fact: A robot arm has an electric motor in each joint that turns by exact amounts a computer orders, so it repeats a move thousands of times without tiring; some place parts to about a tenth of a millimetre.
fact: Robot arms do not see people the way we do, so they work behind fences or with sensors that stop them when someone comes close.
fact: Food-factory workers wear hairnets, white coats and gloves, wash their hands and often walk through a shoe-cleaning bath, so no hair or germs get into the food.
fact: A quality-check camera photographs every item, a computer compares it with a perfect example, and a puff of air or a flap pushes bad ones off the line; some check more than a thousand items a minute.
fact: A metal detector at the end of a food line throws out any packet with even a tiny bit of metal, and staff test it every few hours by sending a packet with a small metal ball through on purpose.
fact: A forklift truck lifts pallets on two steel forks, and a heavy counterweight at the back, which can weigh as much as a small car, stops it tipping forward; forklifts are quiet and turn sharply, so near them people keep to the marked walkways and never stand under a raised load.
fact: A barcode is a row of black and white lines standing for a number that a computer looks up; the first product scanned at a shop till by barcode was a pack of chewing gum in 1974.
fact: Many factories run day and night, so one shift of workers hands over to the next; some glass and steel furnaces are never switched off for years, because cooling could crack them.
fact: In a big warehouse a computer remembers which numbered shelf every box is on, and heavy loads go at the bottom so nothing falls; some warehouses are as big as dozens of football pitches.
exercise: Why does a factory carry things on a belt past the workers? | So each worker or machine does one job in order without walking about | Because workers are not allowed to walk about inside factories at all | So the things can cool down on the long ride | Because a belt makes the food taste better
exercise: What did Henry Ford's moving line do in 1913? | It cut the time to build a car from about 12 hours to about 1 and a half | It made the first car that could fly over traffic | It built every car with no workers at all | It made each car take twice as long but better
exercise: Why do workers near belts tie back long hair? | Belts and rollers can catch hair and pull it in | Loose hair makes the belt slip and run more slowly | Long hair sets off the metal detector by the door | So the checking cameras can see their faces clearly
exercise: Why does an emergency stop button stay pressed in? | So the machine cannot start again until someone checks the problem | Because it breaks after being pressed once | So that dust cannot get inside the button | Because springs are much too expensive to put in every factory button
exercise: Who may press the emergency stop in a factory? | Anyone who sees something going wrong, even a visitor | Only the factory boss, and only after a safety meeting | Only the engineer who built that machine | Only the robot programmer on duty
exercise: What colours is an emergency stop button usually? | Red on a yellow background | Green on a white background | Blue on a black background | Orange on a grey background
exercise: How can a robot arm repeat the same move all day? | Motors in its joints turn by exact amounts a computer tells them | It has strong muscles that never get tired | A worker pushes it into place every time | It copies the moves it saw on a screen
exercise: Why do many robot arms work behind fences? | They are strong and fast and do not see people | The fences stop the robots from wandering off at night | The robots work better when they feel shy and hidden | The fences keep the robots warm when the factory is cold
exercise: Why do food-factory workers wear hairnets and gloves? | To stop hairs and germs getting into the food | To keep the food from making their hair dirty | Because it is the factory's team uniform | To keep their hands warm in the cold rooms
exercise: What happens when the checking camera spots a broken biscuit? | A puff of air or a flap knocks it off the line | The camera beeps and a worker eats it | The whole factory stops for the rest of the day | A machine glues the biscuit back together
exercise: How do staff check that the metal detector still works? | They send a packet with a small metal ball through on purpose | They wait until a customer finds some metal | They wave a fridge magnet at it once a year | They tap it with a heavy spanner and listen carefully for a beep
exercise: Why is the back of a forklift so heavy? | The counterweight stops it tipping forward with a load | So it can push other trucks and big boxes out of its way | Because its engine is made of solid lead to make it strong | So it can drive faster down ramps without its wheels slipping
exercise: What is the rule near a working forklift? | Keep to the marked walkways and never stand under a raised load | Walk right in front of it so that the driver can always see where you are | Ride on the forks if you are small and light | Stand close behind it to help it balance
exercise: What does a barcode stand for? | A number that the computer looks up | The price written in a secret alphabet | The name of the worker who packed the box | The weight of the box drawn as lines
exercise: Why can't some glass and steel works just switch off at night? | Their furnaces could crack if they cooled down | The workers live inside the factory and need the heat | The machines are far too heavy to switch off and on | Electricity at night costs nothing, so they leave it on
exercise: How does a big warehouse find any box quickly? | A computer remembers which numbered shelf it is on | Workers learn every box by heart | Boxes are kept in tall alphabetical piles on the floor | A trained dog sniffs out each box
exercise: A box holds 6 rows of 4 chocolate bars. How many bars is that? | 24 | 10 | 20 | 28
test: «my uncle reckons robots made all the workers vanish, factories got nobody in em now» What is right? | People still design, program, fix and check the machines and run the lines | He's right, no factory has any people left in it | Robots now hire and pay the few workers left | Only cleaners still work in factories
test: «belt's jammed, just reach in and yank the box out quick» What should a worker do? | Stop the machine with the stop button first and never reach in | Reach in fast while the belt is still moving slowly, it's quicker | Kick the belt until it starts moving again | Pour water on the rollers to free the box
test: On a school trip to a biscuit factory, Sam sees a machine smoking and nobody near it. What may Sam do? | Press the emergency stop or shout for a worker straight away | Nothing, visitors must never touch a stop button, whatever happens | Open the machine and look inside for the fire | Film it and wait to see what happens next
test: What would happen if the stop button popped straight back up after being pressed? | The machine could start again while someone was still fixing it | The machine would start working faster | Nothing at all would be different | The machine would switch itself off for ever and never start again
test: «found a hair in me crisps, gross. how'd that even happen, they got nets» What is the best answer? | It is rare thanks to nets and checks, and the factory wants to know so it can fix it | Every packet gets a hair on purpose for luck | Potatoes grow hairs, and some stay on | The metal detector shakes hairs loose into bags
test: In a cartoon a robot arm waves hello to a worker and politely steps aside. What is the truth? | Real robot arms do not see people, so fences and sensors keep people safe | Real robot arms always notice and greet people | Robot arms are too weak to hurt anyone | Robot arms only move when someone talks to them
test: «why'd they put a metal ball in a packet on purpose, are they thick or what?» What is right? | It is a test to prove the metal detector still throws bad packets out | It is a prize for a lucky customer to find | It makes the packet heavy enough for the scales | It is a little mistake that the workers keep making every day on the line
test: The checking camera looks at 1,000 biscuits a minute. How many does it check in 3 minutes? | 3,000 | 1,003 | 300 | 30,000
test: «forklift's quiet, it'll beep if it gets near me, no worries» What is right? | Forklifts are quiet and turn sharply, so keep to the walkways | Forklifts always stop by themselves when a person walks near them | Forklifts are too slow to hurt anyone | Forklifts only drive when the factory is empty
test: A lorry with 6 wheels and a forklift with 4 wheels stand in the yard. How many wheels altogether? | 10 | 12 | 24 | 8
test: «first thing ever beeped at a till was a banana innit» What is right? | No, it was a pack of chewing gum in 1974 | Yes, a banana in about 1950, at a London shop | No, it was a tin of baked beans in about 1990 | No, it was a newspaper at a station in about 1960
test: «mate says Henry Ford's line made cars slower but nicer» What is right? | No, it cut building time from about 12 hours to about an hour and a half | Yes, a car then took a week instead of a day | Yes, each car was then built by one worker alone | No, it made a whole car in about one minute
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of a conveyor belt within a factory? | It transports each item past successive machines and workers in a fixed order | It stores finished goods for a period of several years | It generates the electricity required by the factory | It enables workers to travel between separate buildings
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an emergency stop button remains depressed after activation. | It prevents the machine from restarting until the fault has been inspected | It indicates that the button has ceased to function | It conserves the electricity consumed by the button | It permits the machine to continue at reduced speed
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the counterweight of a forklift truck. | It prevents the vehicle from tipping forward when a load is raised | It enables the vehicle to attain a greater speed upon level ground | It serves exclusively as a seat for the driver | It is employed to crush defective goods
exam: Ascertain the aggregate number of chocolate bars contained in five boxes, it being given that each box contains twelve bars. | Sixty | Seventeen | Fifty | Seventy-two
